.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
Optional physical motion for a \fBSprite\fP or \fBPart\fP, using seconds\&. 


.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
\fBAPI Stability: 2 — Evolving\fP\&. This API is implemented and supported, but its contract is still settling\&. Compatibility is preserved where practical; breaking changes are documented in release notes\&. See \fBAPI stability\fP\&.
.RE
.PP
Create through sprite\&.setupPhysicsBody(mass, momentOfInertia) or the matching \fBPart\fP method\&. Their physics property returns the body, or shared \fBpdg\&.PhysicsBody\&.NoPhysics\fP when absent\&. Queries do not allocate\&. NoPhysics safely ignores physical commands, with a diagnostic in debug builds\&. The physics property is read-only: callers can operate on the returned body but cannot replace it by assignment\&. Use setupPhysicsBody()/removePhysicsBody() to change the association\&. Use identity comparison with \fBpdg\&.PhysicsBody\&.NoPhysics\fP to test absence\&. The pdg\&.NoPhysics export refers to the same singleton\&. \fBgetSolver()\fP returns a physicsSolver_ constant\&. Layers and \fBAnimated\fP have no physics property or physical methods\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const body = sprite\&.setupPhysicsBody(2, 4);
body\&.applyImpulse(new pdg\&.Vector(4, 0)); // immediate momentum change
body\&.applyForce(new pdg\&.Vector(8, 0), 0\&.25); // force for a quarter second
body\&.applyAngularImpulse(2);
body\&.applyTorque(8, 0\&.25);
.fi
.PP

.PP
Linear impulse changes velocity by impulse/mass; angular impulse changes angular velocity by impulse/momentOfInertia\&. Force and torque integrate over a finite duration, in floating-point seconds\&. Zero duration contributes nothing\&. Continuous loads have explicit add/remove methods\&. Physical linear/angular damping is an exponential rate per second; contact friction and restitution are separate\&.

.PP
State and vectors use owning-layer/world coordinates, radians and seconds\&. An optional application \fBPoint\fP contributes angular response around the center of mass\&. Off-center force captures the lever arm at submission\&. In JavaScript the optional worldPoint follows durationSeconds and delaySeconds in \fBapplyForce()\fP\&.

.PP
\fBSprite\fP bodies use Chipmunk when its layer enables it, otherwise PDG's basic integrator\&. Unbound \fBPart\fP bodies also join the Chipmunk world on a physics-enabled layer; otherwise they use the basic solver\&. Optional \fBCollider\fP geometry supplies contacts for \fBSprite\fP and \fBPart\fP bodies alike\&. The basic solver adds approximate linear/angular contact impulses, Coulomb friction, penetration correction and body-to-body constraints\&. Chipmunk uses its native shapes and constraints\&. Free motion and timed loads remain analytically integrated by the basic solver\&. Numerical trajectories can differ with timestep and solver settings\&.

.PP
Owners advance their bodies automatically\&. removePhysicsBody() returns the owner to NoPhysics and detaches retained body references\&. A detached basic body can be advanced explicitly with step(seconds)\&. Dynamic bodies reject programmed movement/spin on their owner; immediate transform setters teleport\&. Growth and stretching remain \fBAnimated\fP operations\&. Set inertia explicitly when appropriate\&.

.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBSprite\fP 

.PP
\fBPart\fP 

.PP
\fBAnimated\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SH "Member Function Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "uint addContinuousForce (\fBVector\fP force)"

.PP
Apply continuous force\&. Begins immediately and continues until removed, cleared, or an ordinary body leaves dynamic mode\&. Assembly loads follow the separate rules below\&.

.PP
For active \fBSprite\fP rigs, see \fBphysics_assembly_loads\fP\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIforce\fP Finite force vector in owning-layer axes, in mass times distance per second squared\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
A nonzero force ID, or physicsForce_None (0) for a non-Dynamic recipient/absent body\&. An assembly uses its selected root as recipient\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBExample\fP
.RS 4

.PP
.nf
const id = body\&.addContinuousForce(new pdg\&.Vector(0, 9\&.8));
// Later: body\&.removeForce(id);

.fi
.PP
.RE
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBremoveForce()\fP, \fBstopAllForces()\fP 
.RE
.PP

