.SS "\fBPhysicsBody\fP applyAngularImpulse (number impulse)"

.PP
Change angular momentum immediately\&. On a \fBSprite\fP using setupPhysicsFromAnimationRig(), routes the impulse to the selected root component and returns the coordinating body for chaining\&. A world application point keeps its lever arm relative to that root\&.

.PP
Angular velocity changes by impulse divided by moment of inertia\&. Only dynamic bodies respond\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIimpulse\fP Finite clockwise angular impulse in mass times distance squared per second\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
This object, for chaining\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBapplyTorque()\fP, \fBgetAngularMomentum()\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint applyForce (\fBVector\fP force, number durationSeconds, number delaySeconds, \fBPoint\fP worldPoint)"

.PP
Apply a force for a finite duration\&. Force integrates over time; it does not change velocity immediately\&. Use the point overload to add torque around the center of mass\&. JavaScript places worldPoint after durationSeconds and delaySeconds\&.

.PP
For active \fBSprite\fP rigs, see \fBphysics_assembly_loads\fP\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIforce\fP Finite force vector in owning-layer axes, in mass times distance per second squared\&. 
.br
\fIdurationSeconds\fP Finite nonnegative active duration in seconds; zero applies no force\&. 
.br
\fIdelaySeconds\fP Finite nonnegative delay before the force begins, in seconds\&. 
.br
\fIworldPoint\fP Finite application point in owning-layer coordinates\&. The lever arm is captured when the force is submitted\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
A nonzero force ID for \fBremoveForce()\fP, or \fR\fBpdg\&.physicsForce_None\fP\fP (0) for zero duration or a non-Dynamic recipient/absent body\&. An assembly uses its selected root as recipient\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBapplyImpulse()\fP, \fBremoveForce()\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint applyForce (\fBVector\fP force, number durationSeconds, number delaySeconds = \fR0\fP)"

.PP
Apply a force for a finite duration\&. Force integrates over time; it does not change velocity immediately\&. Use the point overload to add torque around the center of mass\&. JavaScript places worldPoint after durationSeconds and delaySeconds\&.

.PP
 
.PP
\fBLoads on an active Sprite rig\fP
.RS 4
Finite and continuous forces/torques on \fRsprite\&.physics\fP act on the selected physical root\&. The coordinating \fBSprite\fP stays Kinematic and adds no simulated mass\&. A new registration requires a Dynamic root; otherwise it returns zero\&. Driven roots count as Dynamic, and these loads coexist with their controller\&.
.RE
.PP
The \fBSprite\fP owns the returned IDs\&. Root reassignment or detachment routes existing loads to the replacement root; cancel them through the same \fRsprite\&.physics\fP\&. Component registrations use independent IDs and are not cancelled by clearing \fBSprite\fP loads\&. Delays and durations advance once per physics step\&. While the selected root is Kinematic, existing loads age without exerting force and resume if it becomes Dynamic\&. Snapshots preserve IDs and remaining durations\&. Loads surviving rig disable continue on a Dynamic single body; a Kinematic/Static handoff clears them\&.

.PP
An off-center force captures its lever arm relative to the selected root at submission\&. World force vectors and signed torques keep their world coordinates across a \fBSprite\fP flip\&. They are not reflected with the rig's pose and velocity\&. 

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIforce\fP Finite force vector in owning-layer axes, in mass times distance per second squared\&. 
.br
\fIdurationSeconds\fP Finite nonnegative active duration in seconds; zero applies no force\&. 
.br
\fIdelaySeconds\fP Finite nonnegative delay before the force begins, in seconds\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
A nonzero force ID for \fBremoveForce()\fP, or \fR\fBpdg\&.physicsForce_None\fP\fP (0) for zero duration or a non-Dynamic recipient/absent body\&. An assembly uses its selected root as recipient\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBExample\fP
.RS 4

.PP
.nf
const forceId = body\&.applyForce(new pdg\&.Vector(8, 0), 0\&.25);
// Off\-center force, starting after 0\&.1 seconds:
body\&.applyForce(new pdg\&.Vector(0, 8), 0\&.25, 0\&.1, new pdg\&.Point(110, 50));

.fi
.PP
.RE
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBapplyImpulse()\fP, \fBremoveForce()\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBPhysicsBody\fP applyImpulse (\fBVector\fP impulse)"

.PP
Change momentum immediately\&. On a \fBSprite\fP using setupPhysicsFromAnimationRig(), routes the impulse to the selected root component and returns the coordinating body for chaining\&. A world application point keeps its lever arm relative to that root\&.

.PP
Linear velocity changes by impulse divided by mass\&. The overload with a world point also changes angular velocity using its lever arm\&. Only dynamic bodies respond; static, kinematic, and absent bodies ignore impulses\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIimpulse\fP Finite momentum-change vector in owning-layer axes\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
This object, for chaining\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBExample\fP
.RS 4

.PP
.nf
body\&.applyImpulse(new pdg\&.Vector(4, 0));
body\&.applyImpulse(new pdg\&.Vector(0, 2), new pdg\&.Point(110, 50));

.fi
.PP
.RE
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBapplyForce()\fP, \fBapplyAngularImpulse()\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBPhysicsBody\fP applyImpulse (\fBVector\fP impulse, \fBPoint\fP worldPoint)"

.PP
Change momentum immediately\&. On a \fBSprite\fP using setupPhysicsFromAnimationRig(), routes the impulse to the selected root component and returns the coordinating body for chaining\&. A world application point keeps its lever arm relative to that root\&.

.PP
Linear velocity changes by impulse divided by mass\&. The overload with a world point also changes angular velocity using its lever arm\&. Only dynamic bodies respond; static, kinematic, and absent bodies ignore impulses\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIimpulse\fP Finite momentum-change vector in owning-layer axes\&. 
.br
\fIworldPoint\fP Finite application point in owning-layer coordinates\&. Omitting it applies at the center of mass\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
This object, for chaining\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBapplyForce()\fP, \fBapplyAngularImpulse()\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint applyTorque (number torque, number durationSeconds, number delaySeconds = \fR0\fP)"

.PP
Apply torque for a finite duration\&. Torque changes angular velocity according to moment of inertia\&. Only dynamic bodies respond\&.

.PP
For active \fBSprite\fP rigs, see \fBphysics_assembly_loads\fP\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItorque\fP Finite clockwise torque in mass times distance squared per second squared\&. 
.br
\fIdurationSeconds\fP Finite nonnegative active duration in seconds; zero applies no force\&. 
.br
\fIdelaySeconds\fP Finite nonnegative delay before the force begins, in seconds\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
A nonzero force ID for \fBremoveForce()\fP, or \fR\fBpdg\&.physicsForce_None\fP\fP (0) for zero duration or a non-Dynamic recipient/absent body\&. An assembly uses its selected root as recipient\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBapplyAngularImpulse()\fP, \fBremoveForce()\fP 
.RE
.PP

